I completely understand the problem.
Currently, the frontend is strictly forcing the Final Approver to make a scheduling choice, even when the time is already lapsed. Because scheduleOverrideType becomes null (since they didn't explicitly check the "Override" box), the button disables itself.
Furthermore, if they want to execute immediately, we *must* tell the Java backend to officially "override" the old schedule with null so the backend doesn't throw the ScheduleLapsedException.
Here is the exact fix. We just need to update the **"Confirm & Submit"** button logic in **VoucherAuthorization.js**.
### The Fix
Open **VoucherAuthorization.js**, scroll down to the very bottom where the **"Confirm & Submit"** <Button> is located inside the Action Bar, and replace the entire button with this:
```javascript
                        <Button
                          variant="contained"
                          color={actionSelection === "ACCEPTED" ? "success" : "error"}
                          
                          // 1. FIX THE DISABLED LOGIC
                          // Removed the '!scheduleOverrideType' check. 
                          // Now, it only disables if they explicitly chose "OVERRIDE" but left the time blank.
                          disabled={
                            !actionSelection ||
                            !isRemarksValid(executorRemarks) ||
                            isSubmitting ||
                            (actionSelection === "ACCEPTED" &&
                              viewingBatch?.isFinalApproval === "Y" &&
                              scheduleOverrideType === "OVERRIDE" && !finalScheduleTime)
                          }
                          
                          // 2. FIX THE ONCLICK LOGIC
                          onClick={() => {
                            // Check if the original time has lapsed
                            const isLapsed = vidDetails?.SCHEDULED_TIME && new Date(vidDetails.SCHEDULED_TIME) < new Date();
                            
                            // It is considered an "override" if they explicitly chose OVERRIDE, 
                            // OR if the time has lapsed (which forces the backend to override the old schedule to 'immediate')
                            const isOverriding =
                              actionSelection === "ACCEPTED" &&
                              viewingBatch?.isFinalApproval === "Y" &&
                              (scheduleOverrideType === "OVERRIDE" || isLapsed);
                              
                            handleSubmitAction(actionSelection, isOverriding);
                          }}
                          sx={jaStyles.proceedBtn}
                        >
                          {isSubmitting ? (
                            <CircularProgress size={20} color="inherit" />
                          ) : (
                            "Confirm & Submit"
                          )}
                        </Button>

```
### Why this solves it completely:
 1. **The UI is Unlocked**: By removing !scheduleOverrideType from the disabled array, the Submit button will remain active even if the approver ignores the optional scheduling box.
 2. **The Backend is Handled**: In the onClick event, if the time is lapsed, we force isOverriding to true. This tells your handleSubmitAction function to send overrideScheduleLapse: true and newScheduledTime: null to the Java backend. The backend sees this, safely wipes out the creator's lapsed time, and routes the batch directly to immediate execution without throwing the "time has passed" error!
